      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could the body say what fails without this patch? As far as I can see, after a file shrinks, 4KB processes can still read and write the 4KB pages past the new EOF in the last 16KB page through the PTEs they already have: `truncate_pagecache()` and `shmem_setattr()` keep that page and only unmap from the next one, and `vma_slice_past_eof()` (aosp/4312526) only raises SIGBUS on new faults. So whether such an access gets SIGBUS depends on whether the page was mapped before the truncation, which `truncate_tail_p3s` (aosp/4410080) checks. Something like:\n\n```\nAfter a file shrinks, 4KB processes can still access the 4KB pages\npast the new EOF in the last 16KB page through the PTEs they already\nhave, without SIGBUS: truncate_pagecache() and shmem_setattr() keep\nthat page and unmap only from the next one, and vma_slice_past_eof()\nonly checks new faults.\n\nZap those 4KB pages from 4KB VMAs as well, before and after the page\ncache is truncated, like the native unmap, and include private COW\npages as unmap_mapping_range() does.\n```\n\nNit: as on aosp/4409398, could you wrap the body at 72 columns and drop the blank line between `Bug:` and `Change-Id:`?",
